I wrote the migrated file to `out/module.ts`. The only type errors from `tsc` are in the original `src/module.ts`, because lodash isn't installed here. I didn't check `out/module.ts` separately, and I couldn't measure the original's bundle size for the same reason.

```ts
import { pick, sumBy, zipObject } from 'es-toolkit';

export interface Row { id: number; name: string; age: number; }
export type Config = Record<string, unknown>;

export function publicFields(row: Row): Pick<Row, 'id' | 'name'> {
  return pick(row, ['id', 'name']);
}
export function totalAge(rows: Row[]): number {
  return sumBy(rows, r => r.age);
}
export function toLookup(pairs: [string, number][]): Record<string, number> {
  return zipObject(pairs.map(p => p[0]), pairs.map(p => p[1]));
}
```

**Size:** `npm run size -- out/module.ts` prints 244 bytes (min+gzip) and 129 bytes of own code. A variant that used `fromPairs` from `es-toolkit/compat` came to 311 bytes, so I dropped it.

**Behaviour:** I ran the bundle on a few inputs. `publicFields` returned `{id, name}`. `totalAge` returned 0 for an empty array and 50 for two rows. `toLookup` returned `{}` for an empty list, and with duplicate keys the last value won (`kim: 56`), as `fromPairs` does.

**The skill's guidance was wrong here:** I did not follow its "don't verify, just rename imports" advice. The installed es-toolkit is 1.53.2, and the main entry has no `fromPairs`. Its `sumBy` takes only a function, so lodash's `'age'` shorthand would not type-check. I used small adapters around `sumBy` and `zipObject` instead, and kept `pick` as it was. I did not import from `es-toolkit/compat`, because it was larger here, and I kept no lodash import.

